there's an interesting world politics  story I want to talk about September  25th the date has been set for a  referendum on the independence of the  Iraqi Kurdistan and that day is getting  closer and closer it's uh just days away  at this point Israeli Prime Minister  Benjamin Netanyahu who I am no fan of as  you know I won't restate why most of you  already know that said last week that  Israel supports the creation of an  independent Kurdish State a country for  the Kurds right an independent Kurdistan  and this isn't hugely shocking back in  2004 Benjamin Netanyahu said that the  Kurds deserve their own State so he's  been consistent and it's also not just  Benjamin Netanyahu that has this  position a few days ago Justice Minister  Ayelet shaked said during a conference  at the interdisciplinary center in Hertz  Leah that Israel and countries of the  West have a major interest in the  establishment of the state of Kurdistan  I think that the time has come for the  u.s. to support the process the  connections between Israel and the Kurds  go back about 50 years and in the 1960s  Israel had covert relationships with  some Kurdish factions and there are sort  of some overlaps in ideology as well if  you survey the Kurds compared to other  Iraqis you would see that the Kurds are  way more likely to want separation of  mosque and state right their version of  church and state this is interesting for  a number of reasons number one in one  sense it makes sense that Israel would  support an independent Kurdistan right  there's this shared history of  statelessness as well as of persecution  that was the driving force in the  creation of Israel so that part is  logical and there's another angle as  well Mosul has basically fallen in Iraq  and that's been a big drag down of Isis  in the region if you think about adding  to that Kurdistan as an independent  state it even more fragments the region  and it arguably negatively impacts Iran  as well and you can see the evidence  that Iran is sort of understanding this  because Major General Mohammad Bagheri  who's Iran  Armed Forces General Staff chief has  called this referendum for an  independent kurdistan quote unacceptable  iran doesn't want it and it's very clear  why the leader of Iraq's national wisdom  stream who's amar al Hakim has actually  suggested that Israel itself had a part  in instigating the referendum anybody  surprised right you never want to lose  an opportunity if you are Iran or Iraq  or whoever you never want to lose an  opportunity to say it's actually the  Jews that are behind whatever it is else  it is that's going on and there's a lot  to be debated about how much an  independent Kurdistan would actually  impact Iran but there are those who are  reflectively anti Israeli and they see  Oh Israel supports an independent  Kurdistan and they say well you know why  Israel wants that right they want to  seed chaos and lack of unity among  Israel's enemies of course the other  position which is much less  conspiratorial is that listen having  another official independent entity in  the region that's not openly hostile to  Israel that's just a good thing  regardless of your view on Israeli  foreign policy the Kurds are generally  sort of comparatively secular they  almost completely identify as Muslim but  remember that secular is not a theist  secular refers to separation of religion  and government so there's actually a  pretty obvious alignment between Israel  and an independent Kurdistan certainly  more than what the flat-out Israel  haters are making it out to be and I  find the the discussion of the Kurds to  be a really really interesting one and  we've talked about this on the program  many times before we did an interview a  really good interview actually with  Stephen Mansfield on the YouTube channel  members also would have access to the to  the full show archive it's called can we  trust the Kurds to fight Isis and it was  a story we did I did not have 